# Source verification, EFMP-302 Unit 5 - G3 run agent-g3-efmp302-u5-run001
# Method: Crossref bibliographic record for every key, plus the source's OWN text where obtainable
# (OpenAlex abstract, publisher/PMC full text, ERIC full-text PDF). Retrieval alone is not support;
# each claim below is checked against a quoted passage.
# date: 2026-09-18T20:58:11Z

== maslach2016  10.1002/wps.20311 ==
Crossref: Maslach C. & Leiter M.P., 'Understanding the burnout experience: recent research and its
  implications for psychiatry', World Psychiatry 15(2) 103-111, 2016. MATCHES the unit citation exactly.
Full text (PMC4911781) DEFINING BURNOUT: 'Burnout is a psychological syndrome emerging as a prolonged
  response to chronic interpersonal stressors on the job. The three key dimensions of this response are
  an overwhelming exhaustion, feelings of cynicism and detachment from the job, and a sense of
  ineffectiveness and lack of accomplishment.'
Full text: 'the dimension of depersonalization ... was broadened to refer to a negative detachment from
  work and was renamed cynicism, and the dimension of personal accomplishment was broadened and renamed
  professional efficacy.'
Abstract: 'The majority of this work has focused on human service occupations, and particularly health care.'
VERDICT: topic-01.mdx 'Stress and burnout, and how the two differ' is SUPPORTED. The three dimensions as
  worded in the unit match the source. The unit's scope caveat ('not about teachers') is accurate; note
  the source does list education among people-oriented professions, so the caveat is conservative, not wrong.

== skaalvik2020  10.1080/13540602.2021.1913404 ==
Crossref: Skaalvik E.M. & Skaalvik S., Teachers and Teaching 26(7-8) 602-616; print 2020-11-16,
  online 2021-04-27. The 2021 element of the DOI is the ONLINE year; the unit's '2020, 26(7-8), 602-616'
  is correct and the apparent year/DOI mismatch is not a defect.
OpenAlex abstract: 'The participants were 262 Norwegian high school teachers. The teachers' perceptions of
  three job demands (time pressure, low student motivation, and dissonant value context) and two job
  resources (autonomy and supervisory support) were measured at time 1 (September) whereas burnout,
  depressed mood, job satisfaction and motivation to leave the teaching profession were measured at time 2 (April).'
VERDICT: topic-01.mdx and topic-04.mdx 'Growth and development...' are SUPPORTED, item for item.
  n, nationality, phase, both resources and all three demands are reproduced accurately, and the unit
  restricts itself to the model's structure rather than its coefficients, as the sources file promises.

== little2001  10.1016/S0738-0593(01)00011-6 ==
Crossref: Little A.W., 'Multigrade teaching: towards an international research and policy agenda',
  Int. J. Educational Development 21(6) 481-497, 2001. MATCHES the unit citation exactly.
OpenAlex: NO ABSTRACT. Semantic Scholar: openAccessPdf status CLOSED, abstract elided by publisher.
LIMITATION: the source's own text could NOT be obtained by this reviewer. Support for
  topic-01.mdx 'The multi-grade classroom' is therefore established only at title/bibliographic level,
  which is exactly the limit sources/unit-05.md discloses. The unit's attributed clause ('argued that
  multigrade teaching deserved an international research and policy agenda of its own') is entailed by the
  title; the following inference about its status is marked in the prose as the unit's own, not Little's.
  Recorded as a verification limitation, not as a misrepresentation.

== naparan2021  10.1016/j.ssaho.2021.100109 ==
Crossref: Naparan G.B. & Alinsug V.G., Social Sciences & Humanities Open 3(1) 100109, 2021. MATCHES.
OpenAlex abstract: 'This study examines the classroom strategies of the ten multigrade teachers of East
  district of Tukuran, Zamboanga del Sur, Philippines. ... the classroom strategies of multigrade teachers
  include: Classroom Management, Collaborative Learning, Using Differentiated Instruction, Connecting the
  Teaching to Real-life Situations, Integrating Technology in Teaching, and the flexibility of the Teacher.'
VERDICT: SUPPORTED. 'ten', 'Zamboanga del Sur', 'one district' and the interview method all check out.
  The unit lists five of the six reported strategy clusters, omitting 'flexibility of the teacher'; it says
  'clustering around', not 'the strategies were', so the omission does not misrepresent the finding.

== hennessy2022  10.1016/j.caeo.2022.100080 ==
Crossref: Hennessy S. et al. (8 authors, in the unit's order), Computers and Education Open 3, 100080, 2022. MATCHES.
OpenAlex abstract: 'TPD opportunities in LMICs are limited, unsustained, and often not informed by recent
  research evidence, and outcomes are mixed. Educational technologies offer potential to enhance formally
  provided programmes and informal peer-learning forms of TPD.'
VERDICT: topic-03.mdx 'Pressure to integrate technology' is SUPPORTED with near-verbatim fidelity, and the
  unit's stated limit ('their subject is technology for developing teachers, not technology in the hands of
  pupils, so their review cannot settle Ms Sundas's tablet question') is correct about the source's scope.

== isore2009  OECD EDU/WKP(2009)2, ERIC ED530786 ==
ERIC full text retrieved (HTTP 200, 320727 bytes) and read.
para 17: 'Summative and formative aspects of teacher evaluation are often conflicting - but not necessarily
  incompatible - purposes.'
para 68: 'making trade-offs between different methods is not simple since the advantages of particular
  instruments or methods for summative purposes are generally disadvantages for formative purposes, and vice-versa.'
para 73: 'The relative importance of the summative and formative purposes is particularly contentious.'
VERDICT: topic-02.mdx 'The increased focus on teacher accountability' is SUPPORTED. 'Central design problem'
  is a slightly firmer wording than the source's 'often conflicting' / 'particularly contentious', but
  paras 17/68/73 together carry it. No correction requested.

== hargreaves2000  10.1080/713698714 ==
Crossref: Hargreaves A., 'Four Ages of Professionalism and Professional Learning', Teachers and Teaching
  6(2) 151-182, 2000. MATCHES, and the work is in the course guide's Suggested Readings.
OpenAlex abstract: 'This paper conceptualizes the development of teacher professionalism as passing through
  four historical phases in many countries: the pre-professional age, the age of the autonomous professional,
  the age of the collegial professional and the fourth age-post-professional or postmodern.'
VERDICT ON THE CITED SENTENCE: topic-02.mdx line 67 ('the demand for accountability is part of a long
  movement in how societies relate to teaching') is SUPPORTED by the four-ages framing.
VERDICT ON THE COVERAGE MAPPING: NOT SUPPORTED. coverage/unit-05.md maps hargreaves2000 to U5-07 and U5-08,
  but the only Hargreaves citation in topic-02.mdx is at line 67, inside '### The increased focus on teacher
  accountability' (U5-06, lines 39-75). The U5-07 section (line 76) and the U5-08 section (line 105) contain
  NO citation at all. Nothing in the abstract addresses pay, entry selectivity, autonomy or public
  recognition, so the source could not ground U5-08 even if it were cited there. sources/unit-05.md's own
  'Supports' text for this key describes the U5-06 claim, contradicting its own row mapping.
